<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Somerset</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Somerset is a county of south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> Channel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, with its county town at Taunton. Its low-lying Somerset Levels include the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athelney">Athelney</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> sheltered from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> in 878, and the town of Glastonbury with its ancient abbey. The county was taken into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> in the 7th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/somerset.jpg" alt="Somerset" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puente_Pulteney,_Bath,_Inglaterra,_2014-08-12,_DD_51.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Somerset is a ceremonial county of South West England, bordered by the Bristol Channel, Bristol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a> to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> to the south-east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a> to the south-west. The centre of the county is dominated by the Levels, a coastal plain and wetland drained by the Axe, Brue and Parrett. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendip-hills">Mendip Hills</a>, the Quantock Hills and most of Exmoor lie within it. Its name most likely comes from the Old English Sumorsǣte, the people living at or dependent on Sumortūn, now Somerton. The first known use is in the law code of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex">Ine of Wessex</a>, who reigned from 688 to 726.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The caves of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendip-hills">Mendip Hills</a> were settled in the Palaeolithic. Bones from Gough's Cave at Cheddar Gorge have been dated to 12,000 BCE, and a complete skeleton known as Cheddar Man dates from 7150 BCE. The Sweet Track, one of the oldest known engineered roadways in the world, was built across the Levels in 3807 or 3806 BCE. The Second Legion Augusta invaded Somerset from the south-east in 47 CE, and the county remained Roman until about 409 CE. Roman remains include the baths that gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> its name.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> left, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> controlled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by 600 CE, but Somerset was still in British hands. It was later brought into the West Saxon kingdom, and the Saxon royal palace at Cheddar was used several times in the 10th century to host the Witenagemot. In the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the wealth of the county paid for grand rebuilding of its monasteries and parish churches. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glastonbury-abbey">Glastonbury Abbey</a> was particularly important and claimed to house the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-arthur">King Arthur</a> and Guinevere.</p><p>Somerset was largely Parliamentarian in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, with the Sieges of Taunton and the Battle of Langport among its engagements. In 1685 the Monmouth Rebellion ended at the Battle of Sedgemoor at Westonzoyland, the last pitched battle fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Arthur Wellesley took his title of Duke of Wellington from the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The county town is Taunton, which took the status from Somerton, which had taken it from Ilchester, about 1366. Somerset has two cities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a>, and the largest settlement is Bath, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> known for its Georgian architecture. Its boundaries are largely unchanged since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In 1373 Edward III made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> a county of its own, separate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a> and Somerset.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Somerset” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wessex was a farming kingdom of chalk downland, fertile river valleys and, in the west, the heavier lands of <b>Somerset</b> and Devon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the northern counties the song was often called the &quot;Ten Days of Christmas&quot;, as there were only ten gifts; it was also known in <b>Somerset</b>, Dorset and elsewhere in England, and became widely popular in the United States and Canada.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-twelve-days-of-christmas">The Twelve Days of Christmas</a></li><li><blockquote>…a version close to the famous one, with a familiar chorus but with the song &quot;Christmas Is Coming&quot; as its verses, and in 1973 Amy Ford of Low Ham, <b>Somerset</b>, sang a version called &quot;The Singers Make Bold&quot; to Bob and Jacqueline Patten, again with a similar chorus.</blockquote><a href="/songs/we-wish-you-a-merry-christmas">We Wish You a Merry Christmas</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Wars of the Roses, a Yorkist army under John Neville, Baron Montagu, defeated a Lancastrian army under Henry Beaufort, Duke of <b>Somerset</b>, at Hedgeley Moor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-25">25 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal troops routed the Duke of Monmouth's rebel army at Sedgemoor in <b>Somerset</b>, often called the last pitched battle on English soil.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-6">6 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The judge held that James <b>Somerset</b>, an enslaved man brought to England, could not be forcibly removed abroad.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-22">22 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
